														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858030">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Drama</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> Standard containment procedures, description, discovery, testing logs and containment report.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A sculpture of &quot;The Three Graces&quot; that spreads an memetic infection of emotional empathy. It was commissioned from an anartist for the Manna Charitable Foundation as an attempt to increase charitable acts. In particular, acts that include helping people normally considered of a lower class or importance. This extends to trying to protect these individuals from harm.</p>
<p>Any close viewing of charitable acts caused by the infection further spreads it to the viewer. The effect can also be spread through images and videos. This allows online spread of the memetic effect though literal viral posts and stories.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> Sites near [liberal city] experiences difficulty treating D-class as disposable and accepting collateral loss of life. An investigation looks into the situation and also finds increased charity efforts and the near elimination of homelessness in a large portion of the country. Further investigation discovers the infection. Originating from the sculpture newly installed in the city's downtown area.</p>
<p>The foundation confiscates the statue and tests antimemes for internal use. The site regains the ability to find D-Class suffering and death acceptable via the antimemetics. Using online bots they deploy these antimemes within disparaging and skeptical comments on the viral posts. Nearly all posts are effected and debased as either attention seeking or faked. This extents into offline instances being 'inoculated' by onlookers heckling and mocking the charitable acts.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Exploring the idea that the foundation works beyond compassion. Thus cannot exist in a world that is dedicated to it.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I'm playing with the idea that the foundation is worried the viral posts could become resistant. Thus they are forced to worry not if they are too immoral, but too moral in their acts.</p>

							</div>

								
					
